<header><p class="st-kicker">duchy · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Duchy of Saxe-Coburg and Gotha</h1><p class="st-meta">1826 CE – 1918 CE · capital Coburg</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Twin Ernestine duchy whose dynasty placed rulers on the thrones of Belgium, Britain, Portugal and Bulgaria.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In the Ernestine redistribution of 1826, Duke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ernest-i-duke-of-saxe-coburg-and-gotha">Ernest I</a> of Saxe-Coburg-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saalfeld">Saalfeld</a> gave up Saalfeld and received <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gotha">Gotha</a>, forming Saxe-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coburg">Coburg</a> and Gotha. The two duchies remained legally separate, joined only in the person of the duke, with separate parliaments.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ernest-i-duke-of-saxe-coburg-and-gotha">Ernest I</a>'s brother Leopold became King of the Belgians in 1831, and his son Albert married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/queen-victoria">Queen Victoria</a> in 1840. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ernest-ii-duke-of-saxe-coburg-and-gotha">Ernest II</a> was a liberal patron of the German national movement and of music. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gotha">Gotha</a> was a centre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cartography">cartography</a> with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/justus-perthes">Justus Perthes</a> firm and of insurance, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coburg">Coburg</a> was a court town. Victoria's son Alfred and grandson <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-edward-duke-of-saxe-coburg-and-gotha">Charles Edward</a> later succeeded as dukes.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-edward-duke-of-saxe-coburg-and-gotha">Charles Edward</a> abdicated in November 1918. The <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/free-state-of-coburg">Free State of Coburg</a> voted to join <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a> in 1920, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gotha">Gotha</a> joined the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/free-state-of-thuringia">Free State of Thuringia</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/duchy-of-saxe-coburg-and-gotha.jpg" alt="Duchy of Saxe-Coburg and Gotha" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: David Liuzzo · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flagge_Herzogtum_Sachsen-Coburg-Gotha_(1826-1911).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1826 CE – 1918 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>duchy</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Coburg</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Ernest I, Ernest II, Charles Edward</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Lutheranism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>German</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Sachsen-Coburg und Gotha</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1872&amp;focus=duchy-of-saxe-coburg-and-gotha" aria-label="Duchy of Saxe-Coburg and Gotha on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4931 431 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Duchy of Saxe-Coburg and Gotha lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5251 687L5244 667M5251 687L5251 687M5251 687L5199 691M5251 687L5300 622M5251 687L5251 687"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5244 667h0M5251 687h0M5199 691h0M5300 622h0M5251 687h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5251 687h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5251 687h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Coburg. <a href="/atlas?year=1872&amp;focus=duchy-of-saxe-coburg-and-gotha">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
